<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">12 October in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 12 October, from 539 BCE to 2002.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/539-bce">539 BCE</a> <span class="tih-ago">2,564 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Persian forces of Cyrus the Great enter Babylon</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, the Persian forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> entered the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, ending the Chaldean, or Neo-Babylonian, Empire. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicles</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a> described the city taken without battle; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> reported a siege.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/633">633 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,393 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Cadwallon and Penda defeat and kill Edwin at Hatfield Chase</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Near Doncaster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadwallon-ap-cadfan">Cadwallon ap Cadfan</a> of Gwynedd and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a> defeated and killed Edwin, the Christian king of Northumbria. Northumbria split into its two kingdoms of Deira and Bernicia, and Cadwallon ravaged it for a year.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase">Battle of Hatfield Chase</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin of Northumbria</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadwallon-ap-cadfan">Cadwallon ap Cadfan</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1398">1398 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">628 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Vytautas cedes Samogitia to the Teutonic Knights at Salynas</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On an islet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> River, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas the Great</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and Grand Master Konrad von Jungingen signed the Treaty of Salynas, promising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> for the third time. The dispute dragged on until 1422.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1492">1492 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">534 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Columbus makes landfall in the Bahamas</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After 33 days crossing the Atlantic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s expedition landed on an island its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> inhabitants called Guanahani. The encounter began sustained contact between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, with vast consequences for both.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1810">1810 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">216 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Crown Prince Ludwig's wedding gives rise to Oktoberfest</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Crown Prince Ludwig of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> married Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therese-of-saxe-hildburghausen">Therese of Saxe-Hildburghausen</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>'s citizens were invited to celebrate on the fields before the city gates. The festivities, crowned by a horse race, became the annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oktoberfest">Oktoberfest</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1822">1822 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">204 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pedro I is acclaimed Emperor of Brazil</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, Prince Pedro was acclaimed constitutional Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, five weeks after declaring independence from Portugal. Brazil became the only lasting monarchy in independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1915">1915 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">111 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Germans execute the British nurse Edith Cavell</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A German firing squad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> executed Edith Cavell, who had helped some 200 Allied soldiers escape occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. Her death caused international outrage and became a powerful theme of Allied propaganda.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/2002">2002 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">24 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bombings in Bali kill 202 people</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Militants of Jemaah Islamiyah bombed two nightclubs in the tourist district of Kuta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, killing 202 people from 21 countries. It remains the deadliest terrorist attack in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s history.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 12 October <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dmitry-donskoy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Dmitry Donskoy</span><span class="tp">1350–1389</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/asakura-yoshikage"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/asakura-yoshikage.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Asakura Yoshikage</span><span class="tp">1533–1573</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jonathan-trumbull"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jonathan-trumbull.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Jonathan Trumbull</span><span class="tp">1710–1785</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/kullervo-manner"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kullervo-manner.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Kullervo Manner</span><span class="tp">1880–1939</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/christopher-soames"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/christopher-soames.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Christopher Soames</span><span class="tp">1920–1987</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 12 October <span class="n">6</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/demosthenes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Demosthenes</span><span class="tp">384–322 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/al-muti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-muti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">al-Muti</span><span class="tp">914–974</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-ribault"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jean-ribault.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Jean Ribault</span><span class="tp">1520–1565</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maximilian-ii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="tp">1527–1576</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/robert-e-lee.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Robert E. Lee</span><span class="tp">1807–1870</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/gillo-pontecorvo"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Gillo Pontecorvo</span><span class="tp">1919–2006</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
